{% import "components/ui.njk" as ui %}

{{ ui.hero(heroConfig) | safe }}
      <p class="lede">
        A living list of precise clarifications, corrections, and “gotchas”.
        Corrections follow the process in the <a href="{{ '/methodology/' | url }}#corrections">methodology</a>; for a live incident start with the <a href="{{ '/troubleshooting/' | url }}">first-15-minutes playbook</a>. Treat this as pre‑flight checks before promising
        <em>exactly‑once</em> or “zero data loss.”
      </p>

      {% call ui.panel({ id: 'exactly-once', titleId: 'exactly-once-title', title: 'End‑to‑end <em>exactly‑once</em> vs. <em>effectively‑once</em>' }) %}
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Source connectors are at‑least‑once</strong> by default.
            Kafka Connect has an opt‑in exactly‑once mode for source
            connectors (Debezium documents it from 3.3), but it covers only
            the hop into Kafka and comes with open caveats; see
            <a href="{{ '/exactly-once/' | url }}#per-hop">exactly‑once, hop by hop</a>,
            and <a href="{{ '/is-cdc-exactly-once/' | url }}">Is CDC exactly-once? Per hop</a>
            for how to scope a vendor's claim.
            Either way, achieve <em>effectively‑once</em> at the sink by making
            it idempotent (UPSERT on stable keys, ordered by log position)
            and/or <em>deduplicating</em> using a durable
            <code>event_id</code> ledger.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Transaction boundaries</strong>: Database transactions map
            to change streams, not to sink transactions unless your pipeline
            coordinates them. Don’t assume cross‑topic atomicity.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Per‑key ordering only</strong>: Ordering is guaranteed per
            partition key (primary/business key). <em>Cross‑entity</em> ordering
            is not guaranteed and shouldn’t be relied upon.
          </li>
        </ul>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.panel({ id: 'snapshots', titleId: 'snapshots-title', title: 'Snapshots, backfills, and replays' }) %}
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Initial snapshots</strong> can interleave with live changes;
            design consumers to reconcile by <strong>log position</strong>:
            key the sink on the primary key and apply a change only if its
            source log position (LSN, SCN or binlog position) is greater than
            the one stored. Do not use <code>op_ts</code>, <code>ts_ms</code>
            or any other timestamp as the version; timestamps tie and can run
            backwards.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Incremental snapshots</strong> (signal‑based) are powerful
            but can produce duplicates; always merge on the primary key and
            the highest log position, and keep idempotent sinks.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Backfills</strong> are just another snapshot; isolate them
            (topics/headers) to avoid double‑counting.
          </li>
        </ul>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.panel({ title: 'Tombstones, compaction, and deletions' }) %}
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Tombstones</strong> are required for compaction to fully
            remove keys; keep a non‑zero delete.retention.ms long enough for
            consumers.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Soft‑delete models</strong> (a delete marker plus the
            delete&rsquo;s log position) are what stop a replayed older change
            resurrecting a row. They need a retention and cleanup policy so
            markers do not pile up, and a marker must not be purged while
            older events can still be replayed.
          </li>
        </ul>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.panel({ title: 'Schema evolution' }) %}
        <ul>
          <li>
            Always version <em>payload schemas</em> and keep consumers tolerant
            to <em>additive</em> changes. No producer should introduce breaking
            field removals/renames without a migration window.
          </li>
          <li>
            Use <code>optional</code> + default values judiciously to avoid
            “poison” events.
          </li>
        </ul>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.panel({ title: 'Operational guardrails' }) %}
        <ul>
          <li>
            Watch lag/throughput and <code>max.queue.size</code>,
            <code>max.batch.size</code>, <code>poll.interval.ms</code>.
            Oversizing queue without heap tuning yields GC stalls.
          </li>
          <li>
            Alert on <strong>connector task failures</strong>,
            <strong>restart loops</strong>, and
            <strong>earliest‑offset resets</strong>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Prefer <strong>per‑table connectors</strong> for isolation when SLAs
            differ; otherwise group by SLA and update cadence.
          </li>
        </ul>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.panel({ title: 'Recommended dedup ledger', wrapBody: false }) %}
        <pre><code>-- event_id is a stable UUID in headers or payload
create table if not exists dw.processed_event(
  event_id text primary key,
  processed_at timestamptz not null default now()
);</code></pre>
        <p>On ingest:</p>
        <pre><code>insert into dw.processed_event(event_id)
values (:event_id)
on conflict do nothing;</code></pre>
      {% endcall %}

